(4929247, 714214, 29011, '<td valign="top">Perhaps if the strawberry was dark red ripe and looked black in the b/w conversion but as such it doesn''t work quite well converted to b/w. Perhaps a different b/w conversion method might have worked? if you''d like to PM me after the challenge ends I can try a few methods. Quite curious to see if this has more potential in b/w. (no vote)</td>', 'Perhaps if the strawberry was dark red ripe and looked black in the b/w conversion but as such it doesn''t work quite well converted to b/w. Perhaps a different b/w conversion method might have worked? if you''d like to PM me after the challenge ends I can try a few methods. Quite curious to see if this has more potential in b/w. (no vote)', '2008-09-02 23:45:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(4929251, 714405, 29011, '<td valign="top">A very nice visual and creative take. Some might give you negative points DNMC points for the bit of color there. If others didn''t mention it in their comments, it was quite alright to use desaturation in PP here. <br/>The lighting looks good, I like the bit of halo from lighting the bulb from the top.(no vote)</td>', 'A very nice visual and creative take. Some might give you negative points DNMC points for the bit of color there. If others didn''t mention it in their comments, it was quite alright to use desaturation in PP here. The lighting looks good, I like the bit of halo from lighting the bulb from the top.(no vote)', '2008-09-02 23:46:43.000000', NULL, 1),
(4929252, 714407, 29011, '<td valign="top">This is quite abstract in shape , so this fits for me. Only pitty is the frame is sitting on an angle and not on a straight plane with the camera. Being a mirror view? if the mirror was fitted to the frame, Im not sure how it would''ve worked composition wise, so the only trick I see... would''ve been to popout the mirror from the frame or use a seperate frame and mirror pieces to get the perspective straight on for the frame and still get the angle you want from the mirror.<br/>(no vote)</td>', 'This is quite abstract in shape , so this fits for me. Only pitty is the frame is sitting on an angle and not on a straight plane with the camera. Being a mirror view? if the mirror was fitted to the frame, Im not sure how it would''ve worked composition wise, so the only trick I see... would''ve been to popout the mirror from the frame or use a seperate frame and mirror pieces to get the perspective straight on for the frame and still get the angle you want from the mirror.(no vote)', '2008-09-02 23:47:43.000000', NULL, 1),
