(4945126, 712762, 96534, '<td valign="top">Your comment on this win intrigued me, so I took a look at your portfolio. I don''t know if anyone else has a problem with your work, but I absolutely love it, and COMPLETELY agree about the beauty of simplicity. Your work is just that - simple and very very powerful. You are a true photographer, you don''t need a swathe of editorial gimmicks in order to make your statement, you just make it, perfectly. Please don''t change a thing, you''re a true artist. I''ve picked you as one of my favourite photographers and look forward to seeing you flourish further. Congratulations.</td>', 'Your comment on this win intrigued me, so I took a look at your portfolio. I don''t know if anyone else has a problem with your work, but I absolutely love it, and COMPLETELY agree about the beauty of simplicity. Your work is just that - simple and very very powerful. You are a true photographer, you don''t need a swathe of editorial gimmicks in order to make your statement, you just make it, perfectly. Please don''t change a thing, you''re a true artist. I''ve picked you as one of my favourite photographers and look forward to seeing you flourish further. Congratulations.', '2008-09-09 19:31:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(4945572, 714203, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Color Saturation and Hue:  N/A image is monochrome.<br/> <br/>Brightness and contrast:  The white highlights are very bright and compete for attention.  But the blacks aren''t quite all the way black.  I would have liked to have seen a real inky velvety black in the darkest areas, and perhaps a few more of them.  They would help this image really pop.<br/> <br/>Focus and depth of field:  Both are very well executed.<br/> <br/>A little more black and deeper blacks, as mentioned above, would make this image stand out.  As it is, there is a lot of gray here.  Which, is not bad.  But gray doesn''t grab the attention as well.<br/> <br/>Other than this relatively minor point, a very well executed image, and a well deserved finish in the top 30.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Color Saturation and Hue:  N/A image is monochrome. Brightness and contrast:  The white highlights are very bright and compete for attention.  But the blacks aren''t quite all the way black.  I would have liked to have seen a real inky velvety black in the darkest areas, and perhaps a few more of them.  They would help this image really pop. Focus and depth of field:  Both are very well executed. A little more black and deeper blacks, as mentioned above, would make this image stand out.  As it is, there is a lot of gray here.  Which, is not bad.  But gray doesn''t grab the attention as well. Other than this relatively minor point, a very well executed image, and a well deserved finish in the top 30.', '2008-09-09 23:45:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(4945591, 713191, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Color Saturation and Hue:  N/A image is monochrome<br/> <br/>Brightness and Contrast:  The darkest areas are nice and inky black.  Very nicely done.  The brightest areas lose detail, and that would bring it down a bit for me.  <br/> <br/>Focus and depth of field:  Focus is excellent.  Depth of field is very good.  I have some qualms around the "scar" area.  I can''t quite tell if the apparent softness is due to processing, depth of field, or just an optical illusion.  I''ll go with the latter, since I seem to be the only one who sees it.<br/> <br/>I''m a little put off by the white area.  Compared to the rest of the image it looks almost like a fungus.  There are angular bits, but then the whole thing seems a bit soft.  The angles are, to me, a bit of a jarring contrast to the drops; to the point where they compete with each other.<br/> <br/>I really like the texture of the drops and the surface of the tomato.  If the white part were more muted, or not even there, I might even like it a bit better.<br/> <br/>As is, a nice creative take on the challenge, and I think it should have finished a bit higher in the voting.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Color Saturation and Hue:  N/A image is monochrome Brightness and Contrast:  The darkest areas are nice and inky black.  Very nicely done.  The brightest areas lose detail, and that would bring it down a bit for me.   Focus and depth of field:  Focus is excellent.  Depth of field is very good.  I have some qualms around the "scar" area.  I can''t quite tell if the apparent softness is due to processing, depth of field, or just an optical illusion.  I''ll go with the latter, since I seem to be the only one who sees it. I''m a little put off by the white area.  Compared to the rest of the image it looks almost like a fungus.  There are angular bits, but then the whole thing seems a bit soft.  The angles are, to me, a bit of a jarring contrast to the drops; to the point where they compete with each other. I really like the texture of the drops and the surface of the tomato.  If the white part were more muted, or not even there, I might even like it a bit better. As is, a nice creative take on the challenge, and I think it should have finished a bit higher in the voting.', '2008-09-09 23:55:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(4946394, 712859, 97270, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by citymars:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by violinist123:</b></div><hr/><i> I was interested to see what you''d entered after receiving your comment on my image asking ''where is the abstraction''.  To answer your question, it was an abstract of shapes and gradients of light and shadow.  As opposed to, say, a snapshot of a statue that happened to be sitting around the house. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Meow! :-D </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>:-)</td>', 'Originally posted by citymars: Originally posted by violinist123: I was interested to see what you''d entered after receiving your comment on my image asking ''where is the abstraction''.  To answer your question, it was an abstract of shapes and gradients of light and shadow.  As opposed to, say, a snapshot of a statue that happened to be sitting around the house.  Meow! :-D   :-)', '2008-09-10 04:12:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(4948260, 713597, 67145, '<td valign="top">BUGGER .. !!!<br/>its a real pain when you do something in good faith and then get a dq for it .. its such a beautiful image pat .. <br/>i got a dq in the <i>black &amp; white in colour</i> challenge .. i wrote to the site council twice, i think, to get verification on my processing .. the image that got the dq was indistinguishable from the process i was going to put in, but went that step further in my processing that i thought was ok coz the site council said it was .. wotever .. its so long ago now that i dont care .. !! .. but i did at the time ... in fact quite a few emails went back and forth between me and the site council .. nice ones of course .. !!!<br/>this is the image i''m talking about ..<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=651068" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/823/120/651068.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/823/120/651068.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/0-999/823/120/651068.jpg"/></a> <br/></td>', 'BUGGER .. !!!its a real pain when you do something in good faith and then get a dq for it .. its such a beautiful image pat .. i got a dq in the black & white in colour challenge .. i wrote to the site council twice, i think, to get verification on my processing .. the image that got the dq was indistinguishable from the process i was going to put in, but went that step further in my processing that i thought was ok coz the site council said it was .. wotever .. its so long ago now that i dont care .. !! .. but i did at the time ... in fact quite a few emails went back and forth between me and the site council .. nice ones of course .. !!!this is the image i''m talking about .. ', '2008-09-10 18:22:40.000000', NULL, 0),
